    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into matchup exploitation against mid-tier rotations. **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91) headlines our offensive output, benefiting from a +19% boost due to Merrill Kelly\u2019s high xERA and a favorable park environment that sets him up as our primary anchor. We are also leaning into the high-upside potential of **James Wood** (71.14) and **Mickey Moniak** (69.64); both players are seeing massive +14% park surges, and with **James Wood** batting leadoff and **Mickey Moniak** locked into the two-hole, their path to run production is clear. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.68) remains a high-floor staple, leveraging a strong platoon advantage (+14%) against Roupp to solidify the corner infield.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor** (59.86), who narrowly missed the cut behind **Corbin Carroll** (59.77). While **Josh Naylor**\u2019s raw projection was marginally higher, his -7% penalty for poor BvP history and the restrictive park factors weighed him down just enough for the optimizer to favor the speed potential of **Corbin Carroll**. We are keeping a close watch on **Austin Riley** (65.70), whose robust score reflects a favorable matchup against Michael King, though he carries a 35% rain risk that necessitates a contingency plan. Additionally, investors should note that **Isaac Paredes** (65.35) and **Seiya Suzuki** (63.46) are currently based on pending lineups; while both are high-value starts, **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s outlook is slightly tempered by a wind-in factor that accounts for a 5% drop in his efficiency score. We continue to manage our caps carefully, leaving bench options like **Edwin Arroyo** (38.73) well below our 40-point threshold to ensure we don't bleed games on low-efficiency days.",
